5. Development Model
For this software project, the Kanban development methodology was chosen. This agile methodology allows for greater flexibility than a structured methodology, which has been considered due to the anticipated changed requirements in week 8. 

Section written with reference to Atlassian agile topics information (Radigan, 2024) and Kanbantool.com (2026). 
5.1 Kanban methodology
Kanban methodology is an agile methodology that works by organising team tasks as a continuous flow. Tasks are detailed as cards and added to the project backlog/to do section. The cards then progress through different stages/sections as work is done on them. Most commonly these stages are “To Do”, once the task is started it moves to “In Progress”, and once completed it moves to “Done”. This allows the team to control and monitor how many tasks are currently in development and how fast the team is working. 
5.2 Application
5.2.1 Development workflow
As our development has a strict timeline and reduced time due to everyone’s university commitments, the overall development plan (see section 6) has been outlined in a more structured way, including a gantt chart. As some requirements will change over the course of this project but continual deployments that Scrum advocates for are not feasible for this project, Kanban will be used in conjunction with the structured development plan. This will allow for project requirements to change and to ensure the team knows our strict development and university assignment milestone due dates. 

The Kanban development workflow allows for a visualised representation of task progressions through kanban boards. The workflow and tasks in development can be monitored and adjusted to changing requirements and priorities as tasks are completed to ensure the team is working efficiently and the most crucial features are finished. The process of refining the workflow over the course of development fosters accountability and improvement from team members. 

5.2.2 Task management process
Kanban allows for greater team efficiency by identifying work processes that are currently not working for the team. The team must openly talk with the project manager for any process issues. The project manager can evaluate if too many tasks are “In Progress” or if there is a bottleneck occurring during a stage. The current workload and approach can then be adapted to resolve these issues, ensuring development will continue to progress smoothly.

To manage the task processes the project manager will control stakeholder interactions and development planning so the team only needs to focus on the WIP. Allows for change to happen at any time. Tasks in the backlog can be re-prioritised at any time, tasks in progress are still finished. Tasks are written as cards with detailed information. Categorise and standardise workflow visually and in stages. Identify blockers and dependencies when moving tasks to active development. Set work-in-progress (WIP) limits so team members are not being overburdened and workflow remains steadily progressing. This also helps efficiency as they do not need to switch between many tasks. 

Some key metrics used to monitor team progress are cycle times and cumulative flow diagrams. Cycle time is the time taken to complete a task. The goal is for the average cycle time to decrease or stay the same across the development process. Cumulative flow diagrams show the number of tasks in each state throughout development. This visual representation of team data can indicate if there are any bottlenecks or issues in a state for the project manager to review and fix with the team.

5.2.3 Team roles and responsibilities
Kanban can work with existing team roles but adds a Service request manager, service delivery manager, Kanban coach and Kanban sponsor. 
The project manager will take on both the service request manager and service delivery manager role due to the small team size. 

As service request manager they will handle understanding new requirements and customer needs and writing new tasks (cards) to add to the Kanban board “To Do” section. Through communication with stakeholders and the team, tasks will be categorised, prioritised and given a time estimate for completion. 

As service delivery manager they will handle the tasks across their lifecycles until they are marked “Done”. Team performance is tracked from the Kanban board data, through statistics like lead and cycle times, throughput and bottlenecks. This allows for control and knowledge of the team’s quality and speed of delivery. If any issues are found they will be addressed through delivery reviews and backlog replenishment meetings. This ensures that the team is supported for continuous improvements and that the work being done stays in scope and customer expectations/ agreements.   

The kanban coach role will not be specifically assigned as the team is not transitioning from a structured development process, rather any specific Kanban questions will be handled by the Project manager. Similarly, Kanban sponsor is not allocated as our team does not have a complex structure and there is no specific financial or work support available.  

